entry.summary:
OneSignal Setup in React Native (Push Notification) Complete Guide Push notification setup in React Native can feel confusing at first, especially when Firebase and OneSignal are both involved. OneSignal uses Firebase Cloud Messaging to deliver Android notifications, so Firebase configuration is mandatory before starting. Below is the short and correct setup flow for React Native CLI projects. A React Native CLI project already created. Download google-services.json and place it inside: android/app/
Generate Firebase service account json from Firebase Service Accounts section. Create a new app in OneSignal dashboard. npm install react-native-onesignal
Inside App.js: import { useEffect } from "react"; import OneSignal from "react-native-onesignal";
useEffect(() => { OneSignal.setAppId("YOUR_ONESIGNAL_APP_ID"); OneSignal.promptForPushNotificationsWithUserResponse(); }, []);
After adding Firebase and OneSignal configuration, rebuild the application. npx react-native run-android
Old installed app must be removed before installing new build. Open the app once on a real device. Go to OneSignal dashboard and click Test for subscribed users. If device appears, setup is completed. OneSignal push notification works smoothly in React Native when Firebase configuration and App ID are correct. Most issues come from wrong package name, wrong json file, or missing rebuild. For the complete setup including OneSignal dashboard flow, Firebase service account json, rebuild process, and troubleshooting, check the full guide on our this blog: Onesignal Setup Complete Guide
